WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.550
&gt;&gt; Hello, my name is Dan Ports.

00:00:24.551 --> 00:00:25.880
I'm a researcher in

00:00:25.881 --> 00:00:29.135
the Systems Research Group at
Microsoft Research Redmond.

00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:32.420
I lead the Prometheus project
which is about how to take

00:00:32.421 --> 00:00:34.100
advantage of new hardware in

00:00:34.101 --> 00:00:36.865
the data center co-designed
with distributed systems.

00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:38.690
So, what's motivating this

00:00:38.691 --> 00:00:40.970
is that we are now in
a world where there's

00:00:40.971 --> 00:00:42.350
a wide variety of

00:00:42.351 --> 00:00:44.630
customized and
programmable hardware

00:00:44.631 --> 00:00:46.505
available in the data center.

00:00:46.506 --> 00:00:49.490
We're not just in
an era where there

00:00:49.491 --> 00:00:53.000
are commodity servers
connected by a dumb network,

00:00:53.001 --> 00:00:55.595
but we now have
programmable devices

00:00:55.596 --> 00:00:59.205
including programmable
data plain switches,

00:00:59.206 --> 00:01:02.605
smart Nix and FPGAs
deployed in the network.

00:01:02.606 --> 00:01:05.870
So, what that means is that
there's the possibility

00:01:05.871 --> 00:01:09.230
for the network itself to act
as a computational element,

00:01:09.231 --> 00:01:14.035
to run some amount of processing
directly on each packet.

00:01:14.036 --> 00:01:18.470
We're interested in this
from a systems perspective.

00:01:18.471 --> 00:01:20.120
How can this be used to

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:22.955
accelerate distributed
systems applications?

00:01:22.956 --> 00:01:25.835
The key to our approach
is that we're going to

00:01:25.836 --> 00:01:29.205
co-design network primitives
with distributed systems,

00:01:29.206 --> 00:01:30.800
to use this hardware to implement

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:32.600
some new functionality and

00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:35.000
then build the
applications around that.

00:01:35.001 --> 00:01:37.370
So, to give
some examples of this,

00:01:37.371 --> 00:01:38.570
we're able to build

00:01:38.571 --> 00:01:41.435
replicated systems that
are considerably faster

00:01:41.436 --> 00:01:43.040
because we don't have to run

00:01:43.041 --> 00:01:44.690
a traditional consensus protocol

00:01:44.691 --> 00:01:46.760
like Paxos on every operation.

00:01:46.761 --> 00:01:49.730
Instead, we can run a

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.910
simple primitive in
the network that assigns

00:01:52.911 --> 00:01:55.130
sequence numbers to
requests as they come in,

00:01:55.131 --> 00:01:57.800
and then use a much simpler
protocol that's able

00:01:57.801 --> 00:02:01.205
to implement the replication

00:02:01.206 --> 00:02:03.470
without the replicas having
to talk to each other.

00:02:03.471 --> 00:02:05.590
This lets us improve
the performance of

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:10.660
a existing data center service
by a substantial amount.

00:02:10.661 --> 00:02:12.440
We're able to reduce
systems from having

00:02:12.441 --> 00:02:17.470
an 80% overhead for replication
to more like two percent.

00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:21.170
We're also investigating
new applications

00:02:21.171 --> 00:02:24.075
that require more computationally
intensive primitives.

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:27.500
So, for example,
deep neural network training

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:30.530
requires a lot of
communication bandwidth in

00:02:30.531 --> 00:02:33.350
today's highly parallel clusters.

00:02:33.351 --> 00:02:36.440
We're able to reduce this
communication overhead a lot,

00:02:36.441 --> 00:02:38.840
by having the network
aggregate parameter updates

00:02:38.841 --> 00:02:40.160
from the different servers,

00:02:40.161 --> 00:02:42.230
combining them into
a single update rather than

00:02:42.231 --> 00:02:43.550
having the full set of

00:02:43.551 --> 00:02:45.810
data be distributed
all the servers.

00:02:45.811 --> 00:02:48.775
This is able to to
increase the throughput of

00:02:48.776 --> 00:02:53.540
this neural network
training by 3x,

00:02:53.541 --> 00:02:58.450
which will dramatically
reduce training time.

00:02:58.451 --> 00:03:00.800
We're also looking at
other applications

00:03:00.801 --> 00:03:02.355
including load balancing,

00:03:02.356 --> 00:03:04.250
where delivering
different requests

00:03:04.251 --> 00:03:05.970
to different server
is based on the load,

00:03:05.971 --> 00:03:07.560
is able to increase

00:03:07.561 --> 00:03:10.880
the system utilization by

00:03:10.881 --> 00:03:13.370
a considerable fraction
without running into

00:03:13.371 --> 00:03:16.170
tail latency problems and

00:03:16.171 --> 00:03:18.530
network telemetry
applications where

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.240
a part of the processing is

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:22.535
done in the network
switches themselves.

00:03:22.536 --> 00:03:24.260
Overall, what we're able to do

00:03:24.261 --> 00:03:25.850
with all of this is to build

00:03:25.851 --> 00:03:30.890
systems with dramatically
lower orders of magnitude,

00:03:30.891 --> 00:03:33.465
latency, and throughput overhead,

00:03:33.466 --> 00:03:36.070
by moving computational elements
into the switch.

00:03:36.071 --> 00:03:37.670
The key part of this is to

00:03:37.671 --> 00:03:39.890
carefully think about
what primitives need to

00:03:39.891 --> 00:03:42.200
be deployed in the network
versus what needs to be

00:03:42.201 --> 00:03:45.630
done at the application
layer. Thank you.

